================================================================================
  1.  WHAT THIS CRATE IS
================================================================================

A thin WebAssembly binding over cipherbox-pqc-core. Every operation
that the core crate provides is exposed to JavaScript with a
straightforward naming convention. The Rust logic — the KEM, the
hybrid, the file format, the room ratchet — is not reimplemented
here; it is compiled once to wasm and called from JavaScript.

The API is designed so that the JavaScript shape matches the Rust
shape as closely as wasm-bindgen allows:

  - Rust Result becomes a thrown JS exception.
  - Rust byte slices become Uint8Array.
  - Rust structs with private fields become JS classes with methods.
  - Rust free functions become JS free functions.

See the top-level README for status, threat model, and the list of
what this crate does not protect against. Nothing in this document
replaces that discussion.


================================================================================
  2.  INSTALLATION
================================================================================

Build the wasm package:

  wasm-pack build --target web

This produces a pkg/ directory containing:

  cipherbox_pqc_wasm.js       JavaScript bindings
  cipherbox_pqc_wasm_bg.wasm  the compiled WebAssembly module
  cipherbox_pqc_wasm.d.ts     TypeScript type definitions
  package.json                npm package metadata

Import and initialize in your application:

  import init, {
    MlKemKeyPair,
    MlDsaKeyPair,
    MlDsa87KeyPair,
    HybridKeyPair,
    FileEncryptor,
    FileDecryptor,
    Room,
    RoomSecret,
    encapsulate_to,
    hybrid_encapsulate_to,
    wrap_key,
    wrap_room_key_for_user,
    encrypt_file,
    decrypt_file,
    decrypt_file_hybrid,
    encrypt_private_key,
    decrypt_private_key,
    public_key_suite,
    room_auth_verifier,
  } from './pkg/cipherbox_pqc_wasm.js';

  await init();

The await init() call loads the .wasm binary. Every function in the
module is unavailable until it returns. In a bundler environment,
the pkg/ directory is imported as a module and init() runs once at
startup.


================================================================================
  3.  IDENTITY: KEY PAIRS
================================================================================

ML-KEM-768 (default):

  const kem = new MlKemKeyPair();

  const [seed, publicKey] = kem.exportKeys();
  // seed:      64 bytes. Secret. Determines the whole key pair.
  // publicKey: 1184 bytes. Share this.

ML-KEM-1024:

  const kem = new MlKemKeyPair(1024);

  const [seed, publicKey] = kem.exportKeys();
  // publicKey is now 1568 bytes.

Storing the seed. Never store the raw seed. Encrypt it first:

  const sealedSeed = encrypt_private_key(seed, passphrase);
  // Store sealedSeed in IndexedDB or wherever.

  // Later:
  const seed2 = decrypt_private_key(sealedSeed, passphrase);
  // Throws on wrong passphrase. There is no "empty result" case.

Reconstructing the key pair:

  const kem2 = MlKemKeyPair.fromSeed(seed2);
  // Or, if you also have the public key and want to verify it matches:
  const kem3 = MlKemKeyPair.importKeys(seed2, publicKey);
  // importKeys fails if publicKey does not match the seed.

To pick the level from the public key length:

  const kem4 = MlKemKeyPair.importKeys(seed, publicKey);
  // Level is inferred from publicKey.length: 1184 -> 768, 1568 -> 1024.

Memory. Every key pair object holds wasm memory. Call .free() when
you are done with it:

  kem.free();

Without .free(), the wasm memory grows on every key pair creation
and is not reclaimed by JavaScript garbage collection. Rust drops
the keys and zeroizes them when .free() is called, so this is
also the zeroization point. If you never call .free(), the keys
stay in wasm memory until the module is torn down.


================================================================================
  4.  ENCAPSULATION
================================================================================

The sender has only the recipient's public key.

  const recipientPublicKey = /* 1184 or 1568 bytes */;

  const [ct, sharedSecret] = encapsulate_to(recipientPublicKey);
  // ct is 1088 bytes (1568 for level 1024).
  // sharedSecret is 32 bytes.

The recipient decapsulates:

  const ss = kem2.decapsulate(ct);
  // ss is 32 bytes. Equal to sharedSecret if kem2 matches
  // recipientPublicKey's secret half.

If you use the key pair's own encapsulate method:

  const [ct, ss] = kem.encapsulate();
  // Encapsulates to kem's own public key. Mostly for tests.

Validation. encapsulate_to validates the public key
(FIPS 203 section 7.2 modulus check) before using it. If the key
is malformed, the function throws. You do not need to call a
separate validation function.

Implicit rejection. decapsulate on a tampered ciphertext does
not throw and does not return an error. It returns a
deterministic, unrelated 32-byte value. If the ciphertext length
is wrong, it throws.

  const bad = new Uint8Array(ct);
  bad[0] ^= 1;
  const ss2 = kem2.decapsulate(bad);   // No throw, different secret.


================================================================================
  5.  WRAPPING A KEY FOR A USER
================================================================================

Binary form. You have a symmetric key, you want to send it to a
peer who has a KEM public key.

  const blob = wrap_key(recipientPublicKey, roomKeyBytes);
  // blob is a Uint8Array: "CBWK" | version | kem_ct | nonce | aead_ct.

  // On the recipient side:
  const roomKey = kem2.unwrapKey(blob);

wrap_key accepts plain ML-KEM keys (1184 or 1568 bytes) and hybrid
keys (1216 or 1600 bytes). The suite is inferred from the key
length. The wrapped blob is bound to both the recipient's public
key and the specific ciphertext, so it cannot be replayed against
a different key.

JSON form. For interop with a backend that produces the JSON
structure described in the top-level README:

  const json = JSON.parse(wrap_room_key_for_user(pkBase64, roomKeyString));
  // { v: 2, ct: "...", iv: "...", enc_aes: "..." }

  const recovered = kem2.unwrapRoomKey(
      json.v ?? 1,          // version; 1 for legacy blobs
      json.ct,
      json.iv,
      json.enc_aes,
  );

Read the v field. Do not guess. Passing 2 for a legacy v1 blob,
or 1 for a v2 blob, produces a "wrong key or tampered data" error
by design.


================================================================================
  6.  HYBRID KEYS
================================================================================

A hybrid key combines X25519 and ML-KEM, secure if either holds.

  const hk = new HybridKeyPair();          // 768
  const hk1024 = new HybridKeyPair(1024);  // 1024

  const pk = hk.publicKey();
  // 1216 bytes (1600 for level 1024). Share this.

  const secret = hk.exportSecret();
  // 34 bytes: version | level id | 32-byte seed. Self-describing.
  // Encrypt before storing.

  const hk2 = HybridKeyPair.fromSecret(secret);
  // Reconstruct from the exportSecret() output.

Encapsulate and decapsulate:

  const [ct, ss] = hybrid_encapsulate_to(pk);
  const ss2 = hk2.decapsulate(ct);
  // ss === ss2.

Using hybrid keys with the higher-level APIs:

  wrap_key(pk, roomKeyBytes);            // works
  wrap_room_key_for_user(b64(pk), "k");  // works
  encrypt_file(bytes, pk);               // works

  // Decrypt a file that was encrypted to a hybrid key:
  const plain = decrypt_file_hybrid(encrypted, secret);

  // Streaming:
  const dec = FileDecryptor.fromHybridSecret(secret);

Inspect what a public key is:

  public_key_suite(pk);
  // "X25519+ML-KEM-768", "X25519+ML-KEM-1024",
  // "ML-KEM-768", "ML-KEM-1024"

A file encrypted to a hybrid key cannot be decrypted with a plain
ML-KEM key of the same level, and vice versa. The decryptor
enforces this at the header check and throws a specific error.

The hybrid construction is documented in the top-level README
section 6. It is X-Wing style but not byte-compatible with X-Wing.
Do not mix hybrid public keys with X-Wing implementations.


================================================================================
  7.  FILES: ONE-SHOT AND STREAMING
================================================================================

One-shot. The whole plaintext fits in memory.

  const encrypted = encrypt_file(bytes, recipientPublicKey);
  // Optional 3rd argument: chunk size in bytes.
  // encrypt_file(bytes, recipientPublicKey, 131072)

  const plain = decrypt_file(encrypted, seed);
  // seed is the 64-byte seed of the key pair whose public key
  // was used at encrypt time. The level is read from the header.

Streaming. The plaintext is larger than memory, or you want
progress.

  const enc = new FileEncryptor(recipientPublicKey);
  // Optional 2nd argument: chunk size.

  const parts = [];
  parts.push(enc.update(chunk1));
  parts.push(enc.update(chunk2));
  parts.push(enc.finish());
  // Concatenate parts to get the full ciphertext.
  // update() returns a Uint8Array (possibly empty);
  // finish() returns the final Uint8Array and can only be called once.

  const dec = new FileDecryptor(seed);
  // Level is read from the file header, so a single seed can decrypt
  // files at either level.

  const out = [];
  out.push(dec.update(part1));
  out.push(dec.update(part2));
  out.push(dec.finish());

Treat streamed plaintext as untrusted until finish() succeeds.
If an intermediate update() throws, you have partial plaintext in
your output array and the ciphertext is either truncated or
tampered. The crate does not clean up your output buffer for you.

Errors. Every failure mode from the file format spec has a
distinct message:

  bad magic
  unsupported version: 0x..
  unknown suite id 0x..
  chunk_size .. outside allowed range [..]
  chunk N failed to authenticate
  last chunk failed to authenticate (truncation or wrong key)
  incomplete header
  file was encrypted for X but the key is Y
  file was encrypted for X: a hybrid key is required

Every possible split point across update() calls produces the
same ciphertext and the same plaintext. You do not need to align
your reads to chunk boundaries.


================================================================================
  8.  SECRET-DERIVED ROOMS
================================================================================

A room is a group whose members all share a secret established
out of band. No server-side member list is needed; the server
only routes by room id.

Deriving the secret:

  const secret = RoomSecret.derive("chat.example.org", [
    new TextEncoder().encode("correct horse battery staple"),
    photoHashBytes,
  ]);

  const roomId = secret.roomId();
  // 32 bytes. Safe to give the server; reveals nothing about the
  // secret.

  const authToken = secret.authToken();
  // 32 bytes. Prove knowledge of the secret to the server.

  const verifier = room_auth_verifier(authToken);
  // 32 bytes. Give this to the server on creation; the server
  // stores only the verifier, not the token.

  // On every request:
  //   client sends authToken
  //   server checks sha3_256(token) == verifier
  //   server cannot derive the secret from either value.

Argon2 cost. The default is 64 MiB / 3 passes / 1 lane. This
takes roughly half a second on a laptop and several seconds on a
low-end phone. Run RoomSecret.derive in a Web Worker so the UI
thread is not blocked. If you have measured on your target
devices and want to lower it, pass a RoomKdfParams object as
the 3rd argument.

Joining:

  const room = new Room(secret);
  // Each call generates a fresh random sender id.

Sending a message:

  const envelope = room.seal(new TextEncoder().encode("hello"));
  // envelope is a Uint8Array. Relay it through the server; the
  // server cannot read it.

Receiving a message:

  const opened = room.open(envelope);
  // opened.plaintext    Uint8Array
  // opened.senderId     Uint8Array(16)
  // opened.epoch        number
  // opened.index        number
  // opened.signed       boolean

Signed messages:

  const dsa = new MlDsaKeyPair();
  const [, vk] = dsa.exportKeys();

  const envelope = room.sealSigned(bytes, dsa);

  // On the receiving side:
  room2.openVerified(envelope, vk);
  // Throws unless the signature is valid for vk. No ratchet state
  // is touched if it throws.

Persisting room state:

  const saved = room.exportState();
  // Uint8Array. Secret. Encrypt before storing.

  const room2 = Room.importState(saved);

Rekeying (post-compromise security). After a rekey, the room
remains secret even if the passphrase later leaks.

  const bundle = room.rekey([bobPublicKey, carolPublicKey], dsa);
  // Do not include your own public key.

  // Send bundle to bob and carol. It is signed and its contents
  // are wrapped to each recipient's KEM key.

  // On bob's side:
  bobRoom.applyRekeyHybrid(bundle, bobHybridKeyPair, vk);
  // Or applyRekey(bundle, bobPlainKemKeyPair, vk) for a plain KEM key.
  // The verifier vk must be a key bob already trusts. If it is
  // not, applyRekey throws before touching any state.

  // After all members have confirmed:
  room.forgetEpochsBefore(room.epoch());
  // Drop old epoch keys. In-flight messages from the previous
  // epoch can no longer be decrypted. This is what makes old
  // epochs forward-secret.

What a room gives you:

  - Forward secrecy against device compromise (as long as the
    root secret is not also compromised).
  - Replay and reordering detection.
  - Optional sender authentication.
  - Post-compromise security after a rekey.

What a room does not give you:

  - Forward secrecy against leak of the root secret. Anyone who
    learns the passphrase can derive epoch 0. Use rekey to close
    this.
  - Sender authentication without a signature. Without a
    signature, any holder of the root can send a message that
    claims to be from any sender id.

See top-level README section 7 for the wire format of envelopes
and rekey bundles.


================================================================================
  9.  SIGNATURES
================================================================================

ML-DSA-65 (default):

  const dsa = new MlDsaKeyPair();
  const sig = dsa.sign(msg);
  // sig is 3309 bytes.

  dsa.verify(msg, sig);
  // true / false. Never throws.

ML-DSA-87:

  const dsa = new MlDsa87KeyPair();
  const sig = dsa.sign(msg);
  // sig is 4627 bytes.

Export and import:

  const [seed, vk] = dsa.exportKeys();
  // seed: 32 bytes (signing-key seed). Secret.
  // vk:   1952 bytes (ML-DSA-65). Share this.

  const dsa2 = MlDsaKeyPair.importKeys(seed, vk);

Verifying with only the public key (no key pair object):

  const ok = ml_dsa_verify(vk, msg, sig);
  // Parameter set is inferred from vk.length:
  //   1952 -> ML-DSA-65, 2592 -> ML-DSA-87.
  // Returns false on anything malformed. Never throws.


================================================================================
 10.  BROWSER-SPECIFIC CONSIDERATIONS
================================================================================

WebAssembly memory.

  Every wasm object holds memory that JavaScript's garbage
  collector cannot see. Call .free() on key pair objects, on
  FileEncryptor and FileDecryptor, on Room, on RoomSecret, and on
  the rekey bundle when you are done with them.

  If you generate many key pairs in a loop and never free them,
  wasm memory grows monotonically.

Constant-time behaviour.

  The Rust source is written to be constant-time with respect to
  input values. In WebAssembly, whether the compiled code is
  constant-time depends on the engine's JIT. V8, SpiderMonkey,
  and JavaScriptCore each apply different optimizations, and
  those optimizations change between versions. Do not assume
  timing guarantees in the browser that you would assume in a
  native binary.

Web Workers.

  RoomSecret.derive takes half a second to several seconds
  depending on Argon2 parameters and device. Run it in a Web
  Worker. The rest of the API is fast enough to run on the main
  thread, but key pair generation, encapsulation, and file
  encryption are also best done in a worker if you care about UI
  responsiveness.

Storage.

  IndexedDB and localStorage are not secret. Anything you write
  there can be read by any script on the origin, by a malicious
  extension, and by forensic tools. Encrypt the seed with
  encrypt_private_key before storing it, and never store the
  passphrase alongside.

Sharing.

  A public key is not a secret. A seed is. A shared secret is. A
  ciphertext is not. A room id and a room verifier are not. A
  room auth token is a bearer token — anyone who has it can act
  as a member of that room. Do not log it.


================================================================================
 11.  WHAT YOU CANNOT WIPE
================================================================================

Rust zeroizes its own copies of keys on .free() or on drop. That
is only half of the story.

When you call:

  const [seed, publicKey] = kem.exportKeys();

the returned Uint8Arrays are copies. They live in JavaScript
memory, managed by the browser, and the browser does not provide
a way to overwrite a Uint8Array's backing store in a way that is
guaranteed to zeroize it. Even:

  seed.fill(0);

only writes zeros to the view. The old bytes may still be
recoverable from the underlying ArrayBuffer, from a structured
clone, from a Copy-on-Write page, or from a JavaScript engine's
internal buffers.

What this means practically:

  - Keep seed, shared secret, and any other key material in as
    few JavaScript variables as possible, and for as short a time
    as possible.
  - Pass them directly into the next wasm call rather than
    storing them.
  - Do not log them. Do not put them in structured clone. Do not
    send them through postMessage unless you understand that
    postMessage copies.
  - Treat the JavaScript side as trusted only for as long as
    the key material is in a local variable. After that, assume
    the browser may retain a copy.


================================================================================
 12.  WHERE TO GO NEXT
================================================================================

Read the top-level README section 4 before deploying anything. It
describes the trust barriers (unaudited, not ACVP-validated) and
the threat-model boundaries (no constant-time analysis in
WebAssembly) that you need to decide about before using this crate
in production.

Read top-level README section 6 for the hybrid construction, and
section 7 for the room envelope and rekey bundle wire formats, if
you are implementing a peer in another language.

Run wasm-pack test --headless --chrome or --firefox to run the
crate's wasm test suite.

If you have a use case the crate does not cover, the RustCrypto
ml-kem crate is the right choice for ML-KEM and the ml-dsa crate
is the right choice for signatures. Both are usable via
wasm-bindgen or wasm-pack, and both have independent audits.


